#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114973 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7ad52ce-d408-5390-b91f-c855274f39ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"}] :question/id #uuid "32bd6b27-e95d-5de4-941f-309eb0bc61f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4411" :question/problem "Find $AB-CB$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - C &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-I_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB - CB &= (A - C)B \\\\\n&=-I_{3}B \\\\\n&=-B \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/id #uuid "3b1395b6-49c1-5e0d-ac94-c2d6fc242f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4412" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC)\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nABC &= A(BC) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114975 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "7ab158e6-4222-5cab-88a0-85ce74a7d4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4413" :question/problem "Given that $A, B$, and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n1. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n2. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n3. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n$AB + AC \\neq BA + CA$.\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC$, and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}]}
